  "body": "## What changed\n\nThe lathe axis fit claims an axis only when 90 % of the answering surface meets the plain least-squares line, and the #71 robust pass only ran after that gate. A large keyway or flat failed the gate before the robust pass saw it. Example: a 10 x 20 mm keyway 6 mm deep on a D20 shaft drags the plain line 0.57 mm off, about twice the 0.29 mm band. A 50 mm keyway takes 17 % of the surface. Both were refused with \"the model's normals share no rotation axis\".\n\n- New `crates/motion/src/multiaxis/axis_consensus.rs`. It is a fallback that runs **only when no candidate passes the plain gate**, so every part that already fitted gets exactly the same fit, and no golden changes. For each candidate direction, it keeps the facets whose normal lines meet the line within a cut scaled by the MAD (the kept facets' scatter). It refits on them and at least halves the cut each round, down to the sagitta band. The direction is held while the set settles, so a prism's single pencil of lines can't swing it (the #208 plate-with-boss case stays refused).\n- The consensus line must be met by **75 %** of the answering surface (`CONSENSUS_SHARE`), against the plain 90 %. The coarseness and pencil gates are unchanged. It then takes the existing robust reweighting at the same share (`reweigh` / `judge_at` take the share as a parameter).\n- `spindle_law.rs`: the refusal text moved into `refusal()`, so the untrusted (\"biased fit\") message is unit-tested.\n\nDecisions are recorded in `docs/decisions/113-lathe-axis-fit.md`.\n\n## Readings\n\n- Deep 20 mm keyway: the plain line is 0.57 mm off and the consensus fit is exact, with 4.7 % set aside. Long keyway: 17 % set aside.\n- An off-axis lump of 30 % of the surface and a box are still refused by name.\n- **Owner's call:** the 75 % share sits between the robust pass's 50 % backstop and the plain 90 %.\n- A block standing on a meridian passes, as it already did under the plain gate: its walls square to the axis meet it like flats. Turning follows the part's outline about the axis, so the block is left standing, and the note names its share.\n- `axis.rs` is 411 lines, just over the ~400 guideline. Most of the new code is in the new module.\n\n## Pinning tests\n\n- motion (`axis_consensus_tests`): `a_deep_twenty_mm_keyway_fits_on_the_true_axis`, `a_long_keyway_over_a_tenth_of_the_surface_fits`, `a_shaft_with_a_large_off_axis_lump_is_still_refused_by_name`, `a_box_is_still_refused_by_name`\n- app (`lathe_spindle_keyed`): `a_deep_twenty_mm_keyway_on_g54_passes_the_spindle_law_and_generates`, `a_deep_twenty_mm_keyway_off_g54_is_refused_as_misplaced`, `a_shaft_with_a_large_off_axis_lump_is_still_refused_by_name`\n- app unit (`spindle_law_tests`): `an_untrusted_fit_is_refused_as_biased_naming_its_share`, `an_untrusted_fit_never_shows_a_zero_share`, `a_trusted_fit_off_the_spindle_is_refused_as_misplaced`\n\nBoth deep-keyway app tests fail with the motion change removed.\n\nGate: `scripts/gate.sh quick` (macOS): motion and app suites green apart from the known `swarf_pose_continuity` (2 tests, pre-existing). clippy `-p camcraft-motion -p camcraft-app --all-targets -D warnings` clean.\n\nChangelog: A lathe part with a large keyway or flat (over a tenth of its turned surface, or one that pulled the plain axis fit off) is read about its revolved surface and turns, with the set-aside share named; parts mostly not revolved are still refused (#113)\n\nFixes #113\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n",
